Depends on task 1 (`prisma.supportMessage` must exist). Create a new route file, mirroring the validation/auth style of the `POST /:id/issues` handler in `server/routes/fields.js:762-813` (trim + max-length check, `authenticateToken`, no anonymous submissions -- consistent with every other user-generated-content POST route in this codebase).

Create `server/routes/support.js`:
```js
const express = require('express');
const { prisma } = require('../lib/prisma');
const { authenticateToken } = require('../utils/auth');

const router = express.Router();
const MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH = 2000;
const VALID_TYPES = ['BUG', 'FEEDBACK'];

// POST /api/support - submit a bug report or general message
router.post('/', authenticateToken, async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const type = String(req.body?.type || '').toUpperCase();
    const message = typeof req.body?.message === 'string' ? req.body.message.trim() : '';
    const context = typeof req.body?.context === 'string' ? req.body.context.trim().slice(0, 300) : null;

    if (!VALID_TYPES.includes(type)) {
      return res.status(400).json({ error: `type must be one of ${VALID_TYPES.join(', ')}` });
    }
    if (!message) {
      return res.status(400).json({ error: 'message is required' });
    }
    if (message.length > MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH) {
      return res.status(400).json({ error: `message must be up to ${MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH} characters` });
    }

    const supportMessage = await prisma.supportMessage.create({
      data: { userId: req.user.id, type, message, context: context || null, status: 'OPEN' },
    });
    res.status(201).json(supportMessage);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Create support message error:', error);
    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Failed to submit message' });
  }
});

module.exports = router;
```

Register it in `server/index.js`: add `const supportRoutes = require('./routes/support');` alongside the other route requires (index.js:24-31), and `app.use('/api/support', supportRoutes);` alongside the other `app.use('/api/...')` mounts (index.js:212-221) -- mirror the plain-variable style used for `fieldsRoutes`/`adminRoutes` rather than the inline `require('./routes/chats')` style used for chats.

Depends on task 1. Add two endpoints to `server/routes/admin.js`, mirroring the exact shape of the `GET /field-issues` handler (admin.js:146-171) and a `dismiss`-style POST handler (e.g. `POST /field-comment-flags/:id/dismiss`, admin.js:211-221). Place both after the existing `field-issue-flags` dismiss handler (admin.js:260-...), before the final `module.exports`.

```js
// GET /api/admin/support-messages - list bug reports / messages, newest first
router.get('/support-messages', authenticateToken, requireAdmin, async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const rows = await prisma.supportMessage.findMany({
      orderBy: { createdAt: 'desc' },
      take: 100,
      include: { user: { select: { id: true, name: true, imageUrl: true } } },
    });
    res.json(rows);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('List support messages error:', error);
    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Failed to list support messages' });
  }
});

// POST /api/admin/support-messages/:id/resolve - mark a support message resolved
router.post('/support-messages/:id/resolve', authenticateToken, requireAdmin, async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const updated = await prisma.supportMessage.update({
      where: { id: req.params.id },
      data: { status: 'RESOLVED', resolvedAt: new Date() },
    });
    res.json(updated);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Resolve support message error:', error);
    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Failed to resolve support message' });
  }
});
```

No other changes to this file.

Depends on task 2/3 (endpoint shapes). Create a new file mirroring `next_app/src/services/api/fields.ts:1,109-151`'s `apiClient` usage style (own file per domain, same as `fields.ts` being separate from `users.ts`).

```ts
import { apiClient } from './client';

export type SupportMessageType = 'BUG' | 'FEEDBACK';
export type SupportMessageStatus = 'OPEN' | 'RESOLVED';

export interface SupportMessage {
  id: string;
  userId: string;
  type: SupportMessageType;
  message: string;
  context?: string | null;
  status: SupportMessageStatus;
  createdAt: string;
  resolvedAt?: string | null;
  user: { id: string; name?: string | null; imageUrl?: string | null };
}

export const supportApi = {
  submit: (data: { type: SupportMessageType; message: string; context?: string }, token: string) => {
    return apiClient<{ id: string }>('/api/support', { method: 'POST', data, token });
  },

  adminList: (token: string) => {
    return apiClient<SupportMessage[]>('/api/admin/support-messages', { token, cache: 'no-store' });
  },

  adminResolve: (id: string, token: string) => {
    return apiClient<SupportMessage>(`/api/admin/support-messages/${id}/resolve`, { method: 'POST', token });
  },
};
```
Check `./client.ts`'s `apiClient` signature first (same one `fields.ts` imports) to confirm the `{ method, data, token, cache }` option names match exactly -- use whatever it actually exports, don't guess if it differs from `fields.ts`'s usage shown above.

Depends on task 2/3. Create `mobile_app/src/services/api/support.ts`, mirroring `mobile_app/src/services/api/client.ts`'s `apiClient<T>(endpoint, { token, data, ... })` signature (already confirmed: `data` implies POST, see `client.ts:35-48`) and the same type/interface shapes as web's task 4 file, for consistency:
```ts
import { apiClient } from './client';

export type SupportMessageType = 'BUG' | 'FEEDBACK';
export type SupportMessageStatus = 'OPEN' | 'RESOLVED';

export interface SupportMessage {
    id: string;
    userId: string;
    type: SupportMessageType;
    message: string;
    context?: string | null;
    status: SupportMessageStatus;
    createdAt: string;
    resolvedAt?: string | null;
    user: { id: string; name?: string | null; imageUrl?: string | null };
}

export const supportApi = {
    submit: (data: { type: SupportMessageType; message: string; context?: string }, token: string) => {
        return apiClient<{ id: string }>('/api/support', { data, token });
    },

    adminList: (token: string) => {
        return apiClient<SupportMessage[]>('/api/admin/support-messages', { token });
    },

    adminResolve: (id: string, token: string) => {
        return apiClient<SupportMessage>(`/api/admin/support-messages/${id}/resolve`, { data: {}, token });
    },
};
```
Note `client.ts` infers POST from a truthy `data` (no explicit `method` option) -- `adminResolve` passes `data: {}` so it POSTs with an empty body, matching how other no-payload POSTs in this client work (check an existing example like `usersApi.unbanUser` in `mobile_app/src/services/api/users.ts` for the exact idiom used and match it, since the shown snippet is inferred from `client.ts` alone).

Add `export * from './support';` to `mobile_app/src/services/api/index.ts`, alongside the other `export * from './...'` lines.
